WEBVTT

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:47.880
<v Ira Glass>I was talking to some homicide detectives in Chicago. And they told me that, contrary to what you might have heard, most criminals do not actually return to the scene of the crime. Arsonists do. They like to watch the flames. Serial killers sometimes do, but they're crazy. Other kinds of criminals aren't really known for going back to the place where the crimes happened. Like thieves, for example.
<v Ira Glass>我曾与芝加哥的一些凶杀案侦探交谈过。他们告诉我，与你可能听说过的相反，大多数罪犯实际上并不会回到犯罪现场。纵火犯会。他们喜欢看火焰。连环杀手有时会回去，但他们是疯子。其他类型的罪犯并不以重返犯罪现场而闻名。比如小偷，就是这样。

00:00:47.880 --> 00:01:05.870
<v Ira Glass>If you want to see thieves returning to the scene of the crime, you pretty much have to go to Florida. Beautiful, sunny, Florida. For instance, to a Kangaroo Express convenience store in Pomona Park.
<v Ira Glass>如果你想看到小偷重返犯罪现场，你差不多得去佛罗里达。美丽的、阳光明媚的佛罗里达。例如，去波莫纳公园的一家袋鼠快运便利店。

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:12.130
<v Female Teen>I'm literally walking back and forth in front of the store, holding a sign. A big heavy sign, at that.
<v Female Teen>我正真真切切地在店门口来回走动，手里举着一个牌子。而且还是一个又大又重的牌子。

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:13.820
<v Lisa Pollak>And what does this sign say?
<v Lisa Pollak>这个牌子上写着什么？

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:18.110
<v Female Teen>"I stole from this store."
<v Female Teen>“我从这家店偷过东西。”

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:36.620
<v Ira Glass>This 18-year-old spoke with one of our show's producers, Lisa Pollak, who went with a tape recorder to watch her serve the sentence that she was given for theft. In Putnam County, Florida, if you're convicted of misdemeanor theft, which means shoplifting anything less than $300, Judge Peter Miller will send you back to the scene of the crime, to carry this sign.
<v Ira Glass>这位18岁的女孩接受了我们节目制片人丽莎·波拉克（Lisa Pollak）的采访，丽莎带着录音机去观看她执行因盗窃而被判处的刑罚。在佛罗里达州普特南县，如果你因轻罪盗窃被定罪，也就是偷窃价值低于300美元的任何东西，彼得·米勒法官（Judge Peter Miller）就会让你回到犯罪现场，举着这个牌子。

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:42.230
<v Female Teen>Isn't that quite embarrassing? It is embarrassing.
<v Female Teen>那不是很尴尬吗？确实很尴尬。

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:50.780
<v Ira Glass>For 12 years since Judge Miller first started ordering these people to carry signs, the person goes with the perps is site supervisor Kathy Pekrul.
<v Ira Glass>自从米勒法官开始判决这些人举牌以来的12年里，负责监督罪犯执行的人一直是现场主管凯西·佩克鲁尔（Kathy Pekrul）。

00:01:50.780 --> 00:02:00.110
<v Kathy Pekrul>I would say most of them are embarrassed. Really embarrassed. I've have had two or three girls that just cried their way all the way through it.
<v Kathy Pekrul>我想说他们大多数人都感到尴尬。真的很尴尬。我有两三个女孩整个过程都在哭。

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:20.700
<v Kathy Pekrul>OK, now you're blocking your face with the sign, so you can't do that, OK? You ready? All right, go that way first. Do not say anything rude to anybody. No matter what they say to you, don't answer back if it's rude.
<v Kathy Pekrul>好了，你现在用牌子挡住了脸，所以你不能那样做，好吗？准备好了吗？好的，先往那边走。不要对任何人说粗鲁的话。无论他们对你说什么，如果他们的话很粗鲁，就不要回应。

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:32.320
<v Ira Glass>For two hours the guilty march back and forth with the sign. Kathy brings a lawn chair for herself and a book. Enough people get this sentence that Kathy says she is the best-read person in Putnam County.
<v Ira Glass>罪犯们会举着牌子来回走动两个小时。凯西会为自己带一把折叠椅和一本书。被判这种刑罚的人足够多，以至于凯西说她是普特南县读书最多的人。

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:50.060
<v Ira Glass>It was 120 people last year. Occasionally, Kathy says that some passers-by see what's going on and approach her to complain that this punishment seems too humiliating. And when that happens, she tells them politely that she will let the judge now know they feel, though, honestly, I'm not really sure she needs to go to the trouble.
<v Ira Glass>去年有120人。凯西说，偶尔会有路人看到正在发生的事情，并过来抱怨这种惩罚似乎太羞辱人了。当这种情况发生时，她会礼貌地告诉他们，她会把他们的感受转达给法官，不过说实话，我不确定她是否有必要费那个劲。

00:02:50.060 --> 00:03:00.070
<v Judge Peter Miller>Too humiliating? Hey, they want to think that, that's fine. That doesn't bother me. I'm going to humiliate the one that's carrying that sign.
<v Judge Peter Miller>太羞辱人了吗？嘿，他们想那么想就那么想吧。这对我没影响。我要羞辱的是那个正在举牌的人。

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:14.460
<v Ira Glass>This is Judge Peter Miller. A man who has sentenced hundreds of people to carry the sign over the past 12 years. I liked talking to Judge Miller. I like any public official who gets an idea for how to serve the public from the newspaper. Which is where he got the idea for the sign.
<v Ira Glass>这位就是彼得·米勒法官。在过去12年里，他判处了数百人举牌。我喜欢和米勒法官交谈。我喜欢任何从报纸上获得服务公众灵感的政府官员。他就是从那里得到的举牌这个主意。

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:19.630
<v Judge Peter Miller>Some judge up north did it. I liked it, so I did it.
<v Judge Peter Miller>北方有位法官这么干过。我喜欢这个主意，所以我也照做了。

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.430
<v Ira Glass>What'd you like about it?
<v Ira Glass>你喜欢它哪一点？

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:33.670
<v Judge Peter Miller>Well, I like the fact that you're getting-- somebody's out there and the public's actually looking at a thief. They've got caught, and somebody's trying to do something about it.
<v Judge Peter Miller>嗯，我喜欢这样一个事实：有人在大街上，公众真的在看着一个小偷。他们被抓住了，而且有人正在为此采取行动。

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:40.020
<v Ira Glass>Wait, why is that the important part, that the public sees it? Like I would think it would be more important to get through to the criminal, you know what I mean?
<v Ira Glass>等等，为什么这很重要，公众看到它很重要？我以为让罪犯认识到错误会更重要，你明白我的意思吗？

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:48.130
<v Judge Peter Miller>Well, you want to get through to the criminal, and at the same time the public, too, who are out there looking saying, I don't want to be doing that. Not in front of all these people.
<v Judge Peter Miller>嗯，你想让罪犯认识到错误，同时公众也在那里看着，心想，我可不想那样做。在这么多人面前丢人。

00:03:48.130 --> 00:03:49.770
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I see, it's a preventative thing?
<v Ira Glass>噢，我明白了，这是一种预防措施？

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:53.940
<v Judge Peter Miller>Yes sir.
<v Judge Peter Miller>是的，先生。

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:13.340
<v Ira Glass>For the judge, this is the heart of the whole deal. He remembers back when he was young, when somebody got caught stealing it was shameful. People looked down on it. That sense of shame just seems to be gone among so many people who he sees from the bench these days. When I suggest to him that the punishment he's come up with seems kind of biblical, he's got no problem with that.
<v Ira Glass>对法官来说，这是整个事情的核心。他记得他年轻的时候，如果有人被抓到偷东西，那是很可耻的。人们会瞧不起这种行为。这种羞耻感在如今他从法官席上看到的许多人身上似乎已经消失了。当我向他建议说他想出的这种惩罚看起来有点像圣经里的做法时，他对此一点意见都没有。

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:23.580
<v Judge Peter Miller>Well maybe that's what we need a little bit more. There's nothing wrong with a little bit of religion. Nothing's wrong with that. Could there be more of that?
<v Judge Peter Miller>嗯，也许这就是我们需要多一点的东西。有一点宗教信仰没什么不好的。那没什么不对。能多一点吗？

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.070
<v Ira Glass>What do you hope is going through their heads when they're walking with that sign?
<v Ira Glass>你希望他们在举着牌子走路的时候脑子里在想什么？

00:04:27.070 --> 00:04:46.000
<v Judge Peter Miller>I hope that what's going through their heads is, Lord have mercy. I'm being shamed. I don't like this. And I'm not doing it anymore. Because the public can see those people out there walking, and it really tickles them.
<v Judge Peter Miller>我希望他们脑子里想的是，上帝慈悲啊。我正在受辱。我不喜欢这样。我再也不做这种事了。因为公众能看到那些人走在那里，这真的让他们感到好笑。

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.290
<v Female Teen>She's sitting in the car laughing at me. She lucky I'm under supervision because if I wasn't, I'd slap that [? head. ?]
<v Female Teen>她坐在车里对着我笑。她算走运，我正在受监管，不然的话，我会打掉她的[？头。？]

00:04:53.290 --> 00:04:59.720
<v Ira Glass>In fact, the people that Lisa talked to in Florida who walked by this 18-year-old, they definitely did seem to be tickled.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，丽莎在佛罗里达采访的那些路过这个18岁女孩的人，他们看起来确实觉得很好笑。

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.570
<v Female>I mean I think it's marvelous punishment. Yes. I think it's great.
<v Female>我的意思是，我认为这是一种绝妙的惩罚。是的。我认为这很棒。

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:06.350
<v Ira Glass>Like this couple from out of town
<v Ira Glass>比如这对从外地来的夫妇。

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:08.230
<v Male>Paying a fine's too easy.
<v Male>交罚款太容易了。

00:05:08.230 --> 00:05:21.810
<v Female>I wish we could do that up our way in Delaware. I think the civil liberties union would be all over them-- "You don't have a right."
<v Female>我希望我们在特拉华州也能这么做。我想公民自由联盟会找上他们的麻烦——“你没有这个权利。”

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.980
<v Male>I wish they would do it with people on the highway. Especially speeders.
<v Male>我希望他们能对公路上的人也这样。尤其是超速驾驶的人。

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.250
<v Lisa Pollak>Wait, how would that work on the highway?
<v Lisa Pollak>等等，这在公路上怎么行得通？

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:35.340
<v Male>Well, instead of letting them pay a fine, let them walk out there and say, "I endangered your life."
<v Male>嗯，别让他们交罚款，让他们站在路边说，“我危及了你的生命。”

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.890
<v Lisa Pollak>You mean have them stand on the shoulder of the road?
<v Lisa Pollak>你是说让他们站在路肩上？

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:38.850
<v Male>Well, yeah.
<v Male>嗯，是的。

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:41.910
<v Lisa Pollak>Maybe they could have a sign on the car for the driver.
<v Lisa Pollak>也许他们可以给司机在车上贴个牌子。

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:50.640
<v Female>Yes, yes! Excellent.
<v Female>是的，是的！太棒了。

00:05:50.640 --> 00:06:19.530
<v Ira Glass>During the two hours as the 18-year-old marches back and forth, she actually doesn't get that much abuse from people. It's nowhere as bad as she thought. Some cars honk at her. People point. A woman shoots her a disgusted look. Two old men stand in the store, arms folded, and watch her, amused. But most people don't say anything at all. In Putnam County, after all, this is not an unusual sight. They look at her sign. They walk by. And she chats, cheerfully, with lots of people. She waves to a guy passing by.
<v Ira Glass>在这两个小时里，18岁的女孩来回走动，她并没有受到人们太多的辱骂。情况远没有她想象的那么糟。有些车会对她按喇叭。人们指指点点。一个女人向她投去厌恶的目光。两个老人在店里抱臂看着她，觉得很有趣。但大多数人什么都没说。毕竟在普特南县，这并不是什么罕见的景象。他们看着她的牌子。他们走过去。而她愉快地与许多人聊天。她向路过的一个人挥手。

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.300
<v Female Teen>What's up, Boo? How you been doing?
<v Female Teen>怎么了，Boo？你最近怎么样？

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:41.180
<v Ira Glass>Another guy flirts with her. Tells her, "Keep your chin up." Somebody yells from his car, "You should hold a sign that says, I was the only one who got caught." And anytime that strangers do stop and read the sign, or try to talk to her, this is what she says--
<v Ira Glass>另一个人跟她调情。对她说，“把头抬起来。”有人从车里喊道：“你应该举一个写着‘我是唯一一个被抓住的’的牌子。”每当陌生人停下来读牌子，或者试图跟她说话时，她就会这么说——

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:50.770
<v Female Teen>It's OK. I was stealing milk for my little girl. This is why this happened. I was stealing milk for my little girl. That's why I'm holding this sign here.
<v Female Teen>没关系。我是在为我的小女儿偷牛奶。这就是为什么会发生这种事。我是在为我的小女儿偷牛奶。这就是我为什么举着这个牌子的原因。

00:06:50.770 --> 00:07:20.550
<v Ira Glass>Her little girl is one. In fact, according to the police report, she and a friend were arrested for taking the following-- one package of Oreo Cakesters, one Twix bar, one Milky Way, one package of M&M's, one can of Dr. Pepper, and, yes, one pint of milk. But keep in mind, she is just a kid. She's a teenager. She's just a kid, right? So, yes, she's not only unrepentant, just like the judge says he sees from the bench, she's also kind of indignant.
<v Ira Glass>她的女儿才一岁。事实上，根据警方报告，她和一个朋友被捕是因为拿了以下东西——一包奥利奥夹心饼干，一根特趣巧克力棒，一根银河巧克力棒，一包M&M豆，一罐Dr. Pepper，还有，没错，一品脱牛奶。但请记住，她只是个孩子。她还是个青少年。她只是个孩子，对吧？所以，是的，她不仅像法官在法官席上所说的那样毫无悔意，她甚至还有点愤愤不平。

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:30.160
<v Female Teen>See I was the only one that got caught. I was the only one that was stupid enough to let the camera see me. And then them two little boys in there, they're the ones that made me go to jail. Excuse me but--
<v Female Teen>你看，我是唯一一个被抓住的。我是唯一一个笨到让摄像头拍到的人。然后那两个在里面的小男孩，是他们害我进了监狱。不好意思，但是——

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:31.440
<v Lisa Pollak>You mean the store managers?
<v Lisa Pollak>你是说店经理吗？

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:46.720
<v Female Teen>Yes, that would be them, but I don't like them. Them bitches in there, they didn't have to take me to jail. See they chose to take me to jail. Which all they had to do was give us a trespassing warning. That's all they had to do.
<v Female Teen>是的，就是他们，我不喜欢他们。那几个贱人，她们没必要把我送进监狱。你看，她们选择把我送进监狱。她们本来只需要给我们一个非法入侵警告就行了。她们本来只需要做这些。

00:07:46.720 --> 00:07:52.120
<v Lisa Pollak>If this is meant to be a learning experience, tell me what you think you learned?
<v Lisa Pollak>如果这被视为一种学习经历，告诉我你认为你学到了什么？

00:07:52.120 --> 00:08:05.100
<v Female Teen>Honestly? I didn't learn anything. I might sound stupid or dumb, but honestly I didn't learn anything. I'll steal again. This doesn't solve anything.
<v Female Teen>老实说吗？我什么也没学到。我听起来可能很蠢，但老实说我什么也没学到。我还会再偷的。这解决不了任何问题。

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.990
<v Lisa Pollak>Are you worried about saying that?
<v Lisa Pollak>你担心说这些话吗？

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:07.770
<v Female Teen>No.
<v Female Teen>不。

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:08.250
<v Lisa Pollak>On radio?
<v Lisa Pollak>在广播里说？

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:16.740
<v Female Teen>No, I'm not worried about a thing. Because no one knows nothing about it.
<v Female Teen>不，我什么都不担心。因为没人知道关于这件事的任何情况。

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:58.020
<v Ira Glass>The county doesn't keep statistics on how many people who carry the sign get caught again for stealing. But probation officer Kathy Pekrul says that maybe only 20% of the people who she sees carrying the sign seemed totally unrepentant. Of course, what the judge wants is such a delicate thing to try to force on to somebody. He wants them to return to the scene of the crime, and have this poetic moment of realization. He wants them to take stock of their lives. He wants them to change. And you just can't predict when that's going to happen. Kathy Pekrul remembers this one young man who she took to carry the sign in front of a liquor store that he'd robbed in Crescent City. He spent the whole time joking with people and carrying on.
<v Ira Glass>县里没有统计有多少举牌的人会再次因偷窃被抓。但缓刑官凯西·佩克鲁尔说，她所见的举牌人中可能只有20%看起来是完全没有悔意的。当然，法官想要强加给别人的东西是如此微妙。他希望他们回到犯罪现场，有一个诗意般的顿悟时刻。他希望他们反思自己的生活。他希望他们做出改变。而你无法预测什么时候会发生这种情况。凯西·佩克鲁尔记得有个年轻人，她带他去他抢劫过的一家克雷森特城的酒类商店前举牌。他全程都在和人们开玩笑，举止放肆。

00:08:58.020 --> 00:09:07.080
<v Kathy Pekrul>His whole family came out and they kept riding around the block and laughing at him, and stopping and taking pictures of him, and he was posing with sign.
<v Kathy Pekrul>他的全家都出来了，他们不断地绕着街区开车并嘲笑他，还停下来给他拍照，他则摆姿势和牌子合影。

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.920
<v Ira Glass>And he thought it was funny too?
<v Ira Glass>他也觉得这很有趣吗？

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:17.410
<v Kathy Pekrul>Yes he did. And I would have told you, if you had asked me, that he was one of the ones that would repeat. I would have been sure of it.
<v Kathy Pekrul>是的，他觉得。如果你问我，我肯定会告诉你，他是那种会重犯的人。我对此很确定。

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:27.550
<v Ira Glass>Three or four years later, she was back at the same liquor store with another perp, and that guy walked up to her and she didn't remember his name. But she definitely remembered how much fun he had had when he had carried the sign.
<v Ira Glass>三四年后，她带着另一个罪犯回到同一家酒类商店，那个人向她走来，她记不起他的名字了。但她绝对记得他举牌时玩得有多开心。

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:50.820
<v Kathy Pekrul>So we were talking. And he was just-- we were just talking-- and then he said to me he wanted me to know that he was on the straight and narrow, I think he said, or something to that effect. And that he had finished a criminal justice degree and he was a policeman for someone. And this was a surprise. Very surprised.
<v Kathy Pekrul>所以我们在谈话。他只是——我们当时正在交谈——然后他对我提到，他想让我知道他已经走上了正道，我想他是这么说的，或者类似的话。他还说他已经拿到了刑事司法学位，现在是某人的警察了。这真是一个惊喜。非常惊讶。

00:09:50.820 --> 00:10:06.490
<v Kathy Pekrul>So you know, you never know. Because I would have told you for sure he was going to repeat. Basically, it's very much like this girl today.
<v Kathy Pekrul>所以你知道，你永远不会知道。因为我肯定会告诉你他会重犯的。基本上，这和今天这个女孩的情况非常相似。

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:14.670
<v Ira Glass>Well, today on our program, we joined several different people in this very fraught situation. Where they returned to the scenes of various crimes of various sorts.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，今天在我们的节目中，我们和几个不同的人一起参与了这个非常令人紧张的局面。他们回到了各种不同犯罪现场。

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:21.280
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, It's This American Life, distributed by Public Radio International. I'm Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>来自WBEZ芝加哥，这是《美国生活》，由国际公共广播电台发行。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。

00:10:21.280 --> 00:10:37.980
<v Ira Glass>Our program today is recorded at the Chicago Theater in downtown Chicago and at the Skirball Center for the Performing Arts in New York University, where it is being beamed to 430 movie theaters around the country right now.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目是在芝加哥市中心的芝加哥剧院和纽约大学的斯基博尔表演艺术中心录制的，目前正通过卫星传输到全国430家电影院。

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:51.240
<v Ira Glass>From the Brunswick 10 theater in Maine, to the AMC Mission Valley Theater in San Diego, to the Capitol 14 Theater in Olympia, Washington, to the City North and River East and Webster Place theaters in our beloved Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>从缅因州的布伦瑞克10电影院，到圣地亚哥的AMC使命谷电影院，到华盛顿奥林匹亚的国会14电影院，再到我们钟爱的芝加哥的城市北部、东河和韦伯斯特广场电影院。

00:10:51.240 --> 00:11:06.700
<v Ira Glass>We have stories today of crimes in traffic, in a family, in a marriage, crimes with God, Mike Birbiglia, Dan Savage, musical guest Joss Whedon, and much, much more.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的故事涉及交通犯罪、家庭犯罪、婚姻犯罪、亵渎神灵，还有迈克·比比利亚（Mike Birbiglia）、丹·萨维奇（Dan Savage）、音乐嘉宾乔斯·韦登（Joss Whedon），以及更多内容。

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:17.660
<v Ira Glass>Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>请继续收听。

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:23.200
<v Ira Glass>Act One. D-U-WHY?
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。酒驾（D-U-WHY）？

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:37.150
<v Ira Glass>So this guy committed a crime and it had huge consequences for this first person we're bringing out, Mike Birbiglia, and then the best way to describe what happened is Mike found he could not leave the scene of the crime.
<v Ira Glass>所以这个家伙犯了罪，这对我们要请出的第一位嘉宾迈克·比比利亚造成了巨大的影响，而描述所发生事情的最好方式是，迈克发现他无法离开那个犯罪现场。

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.080
<v Ira Glass>Please welcome Mike Birbiglia.
<v Ira Glass>欢迎迈克·比比利亚。

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:56.880
<v Ira Glass>[APPLAUSE]
<v Ira Glass>[掌声]

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:21.580
<v Mike Birbiglia>Two years ago I'm in Los Angeles, and I'm at my friend Andy's house and we're watching TV. And earlier in the night I'd gotten in a fight with my girlfriend, Jenny. You ever get in a fight with your girlfriend that's so bad that afterwards you don't know where you are or what year it is? I was just like, well, let's just watch Bagger Vance.
<v Mike Birbiglia>两年前我在洛杉矶，在朋友安迪家里看电视。那天晚上早些时候，我和女友珍妮吵了一架。你有没有和女友吵过那种严重的架，以至于事后你不知道自己在哪里，或者现在是哪一年？我当时就是觉得，好吧，还是看《重返荣耀》（Bagger Vance）吧。

00:12:21.580 --> 00:12:40.640
<v Mike Birbiglia>She had been at the wedding of one of her friends. And she was annoyed because people she hardly knew kept asking about our status as a couple. So annoyed, that she called me to ask about our status as a couple. And this is problematic because I'd always been against the idea of marriage.
<v Mike Birbiglia>她去参加了她朋友的婚礼。她很烦，因为她几乎不认识的人一直在问我们的恋爱状态。她非常烦，以至于她打电话给我询问我们的恋爱状态。这很成问题，因为我一直反对婚姻这个想法。

00:12:40.640 --> 00:13:07.960
<v Mike Birbiglia>I'd decided I wasn't going to get married until I was sure that nothing else good could happen in my life. Basically, I didn't see the upside of there being one person you're assigned to and who's assigned to you. Because I never looked at my parents' marriage, or really anyone who had been married 30 years and thought, "I gotta get me some of that."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我决定在确信我生活中不会再发生任何好事之前，我是不会结婚的。基本上，我不觉得和一个被指定给你、而你也被指定给他的人在一起有什么好处。因为我从未看过我父母的婚姻，或者任何结婚30年的人，然后想，“我也得弄一段那种婚姻。”

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:32.280
<v Mike Birbiglia>Well, this is something that Andy and I had rallied behind together. And we knew we were right. This is important for me to point out-- that sometimes when I think that I'm right, it can be a real source of contention between me and people who I'm close to. And the reason it's a source of contention, is that I'm right.
<v Mike Birbiglia>嗯，这是安迪和我一起坚持的立场。我们知道我们是对的。这一点很重要——有时当我认为自己正确时，这可能会成为我和亲近的人之间真正的争论源头。而成为争论源头的原因，是因为我确实是对的。

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:58.180
<v Mike Birbiglia>So not only did Andy and I not believe in marriage for ourselves, but we made it our mission to discourage other people from getting married, and people we cared about most. Andy and I stopped or put on hold four to seven marriages. We were pretty good. We weren't amazing, but we were solid.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以，安迪和我不仅不相信婚姻适合我们自己，而且我们将阻止我们最关心的人结婚视为我们的使命。安迪和我阻止或搁置了四到七场婚礼。我们相当厉害。我们不算惊人，但我们很稳。

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:25.930
<v Mike Birbiglia>I'm at Andy's house and it's 1:00 AM, and I get into my rental car and I head back to my hotel. And I make a right turn out of Andy's street and I am T-boned. That's the culinary way of describing it. It means that I am hit, driver's side, at a 90-degree angle, like a T-bone steak. And it was by a drunk driver, who probably would have enjoyed that.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我在安迪家，当时是凌晨1点，我上了我的租车，开车回我的酒店。我从安迪那条街右转出来，然后我就被“T型撞击”了。这是描述这种情况的专业术语。意思是说，我在驾驶座一侧被以90度角撞击，就像T骨牛排一样。而且撞我的是一个醉酒司机，他可能还会享受那种感觉。

00:14:25.930 --> 00:15:00.460
<v Mike Birbiglia>And being hit by a car is hard to describe. I'm sure some of you have experienced this, but you know those water slides where you lie on your back and then you fall at a vertical angle at like 1,000 miles an hour? It's like if you went on one of those, but no one told you you were going on the slide. It's like if you were taking a shower, and then you're on that slide.
<v Mike Birbiglia>被车撞是很难描述的。我相信你们中的一些人经历过，但你知道那种你躺在背上，然后以每小时1000英里的速度垂直掉落的水滑梯吗？这就好像你坐上了其中一个，但没人告诉你你要去滑滑梯。就像你正在洗澡，然后突然就上了那个滑梯。

00:15:00.460 --> 00:15:16.380
<v Mike Birbiglia>So in one and a half seconds, my car spins around 180 degrees and I hear nothing. And I think I'm dead, there's no weight. I'm paralyzed. And then I hear nothing. And then I hear the other car skid out and drive away.
<v Mike Birbiglia>所以在一秒半的时间里，我的车旋转了180度，我什么都没听见。我以为我死了，没有重量感。我瘫痪了。然后我什么都没听见。然后我听到另一辆车加速逃离的声音。

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:39.240
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I have that Elie Wiesel moment where I think, "Human beings are animals." I think that's how he said it. And 20 minutes later, I'm sitting on the curb. Andy had shown up, as well the police and the paramedics, and that's when I start crying.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我有那种埃利·维塞尔（Elie Wiesel）式的时刻，我想，“人类是动物。”我想他是这么说的。20分钟后，我坐在路边。安迪出现了，警察和护理人员也到了，那时我开始哭。

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:57.430
<v Mike Birbiglia>You know how when you drop a baby on the ground? It doesn't start crying right away because it doesn't understand the concept of dropping a baby on the ground. Until it sees your face and then it's like, oh I guess I should be crying or something.
<v Mike Birbiglia>你知道当你把婴儿掉在地上的时候吗？它不会马上哭，因为它不理解把婴儿掉在地上的概念。直到它看到你的脸，然后它才会想，哦，我想我应该哭或者什么的。

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:12.550
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I am crying because I'm looking at my totaled car, and it hits me that in that moment I might have ceased to exist. A police officer walks over to me and he says, "What happened?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>我在哭，因为我看着我的报废车，那一刻我意识到我可能已经不复存在了。一名警察向我走来，他说：“发生了什么？”

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:18.590
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I say, "I was hit by this car and then I heard nothing and then I heard it skid out and drive away."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说，“我被那辆车撞了，然后我什么都没听见，接着我就听到它加速开走了。”

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:30.740
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Well, he didn't get too far." He points to the intersection 100 yards away and the other car has made a right turn and driven into a small tree.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“嗯，他没跑多远。”他指向100码外的十字路口，那辆车右转撞进了一棵小树里。

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:45.670
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I can't help but think, "That is karma, bitch." That is a hit and run, and hit.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我忍不住想，“这就是报应，混蛋。”这就是肇事逃逸，然后被抓。

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:50.600
<v Mike Birbiglia>I'm on the curb, and the cop asked me to sign a piece of paper and I say, "What's it for?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>我在路边，警察让我签一张纸，我说，“这是干什么的？”

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:56.130
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "It's a statement saying you're OK and that we can leave."
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“这是一份声明，说你没事，我们可以离开了。”

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:59.250
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I said, "I don't know if I'm OK."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说，“我不知道我是否有事。”

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:01.780
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Just sign it."
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“签了就是。”

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:05.030
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I said, "No, I actually-- I'm a little shaken up."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说，“不，我其实——我有点受惊了。”

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:14.770
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Just sign it." And he holds it in my face, and it occurs to me that he's not going to move.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“签了就是。”他把纸怼到我脸上，我意识到他不会让步。

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:36.980
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I sign it. And Andy drives me to the hospital just as a precaution. And we had to wait an hour because the doctor is treating the drunk guy. He beat us there. Eventually we're with the doctor and he apologizes for the wait. And Andy says, "Was the other guy drunk?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>于是我签了。安迪开车送我去了医院，以防万一。我们等了一个小时，因为医生正在治疗那个醉汉。他比我们先到。最后我们见到了医生，他为等待道歉。安迪问，“那个人喝醉了吗？”

00:17:36.980 --> 00:17:39.670
<v Mike Birbiglia>And the doctor says, "I can't answer that."
<v Mike Birbiglia>医生说，“我不能回答那个问题。”

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:52.790
<v Mike Birbiglia>And Andy says, "Was he?" He uses the tactic we had learned earlier from the cop. And it works!
<v Mike Birbiglia>安迪说，“他喝了吗？”他用了我们之前从警察那里学到的策略。这招管用！

00:17:52.790 --> 00:18:08.420
<v Mike Birbiglia>The doctor says, "Well, he's heading to jail now." And Andy and I flash each other a look like the Hardy Boys. Case closed.
<v Mike Birbiglia>医生说，“嗯，他现在要进监狱了。”安迪和我交换了一个眼神，就像《哈迪兄弟》侦探一样。结案。

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:27.780
<v Mike Birbiglia>Andy and I get back to the house at 4 or 5 o'clock, and I have one of those cliche revelations a lot of people have when they have near death experiences. And I'm like, I think I have to call Jenny and tell her that we need to get married. And Andy says, "Sleep on it."
<v Mike Birbiglia>安迪和我在凌晨4点或5点回到家，我产生了许多人在濒死经历中常有的那种老套的顿悟。我想，我得打电话给珍妮，告诉她我们需要结婚。安迪说，“睡一觉再想吧。”

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:34.250
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I say, "No, no. I figured it out. I mean this all makes sense. I need to call her right now."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说，“不，不。我想通了。我的意思是这一切都说得通。我得现在就给她打电话。”

00:18:34.250 --> 00:18:47.780
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Mike, sleep on it." He saves me.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“迈克，睡一觉再说吧。”他救了我。

00:18:47.780 --> 00:19:17.220
<v Mike Birbiglia>And the next morning, I fly back to New York and I get a call on my voice mail from the rental car agency telling me that the accident report found me at fault. And I owed $12,000 for the repairs on the other guy's Mercedes SUV. And I called back right away, and I explained that this is a mistake and the woman says, "Unless they change the accident report, you owe $12,000."
<v Mike Birbiglia>第二天早上，我飞回纽约，在语音信箱里收到租车公司的电话，告诉我事故报告显示是我方的错。我欠了12000美元来修理那个家伙的梅赛德斯SUV。我马上打回去，解释说这是一个错误，但那个女人说，“除非他们修改事故报告，否则你得赔12000美元。”

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:45.760
<v Mike Birbiglia>And so I just start freaking out. And I'm like, I need to fix this. I get the accident report and it's a mess. I can see why there's a misunderstanding. An accident report is kind of like homework for cops. And Officer Timson, not so good with the homework. The report mixes up vehicle one and vehicle two, driver one and driver two. I actually want to show you this.
<v Mike Birbiglia>于是我开始崩溃。我心想，我得解决这个问题。我拿到了事故报告，简直一团糟。我能看出为什么会有误解。事故报告对警察来说就像家庭作业一样。而蒂姆森警官（Officer Timson），不太擅长做作业。报告把车辆一和车辆二、驾驶员一和驾驶员二混淆了。我真的很想给你们看看这个。

00:19:45.760 --> 00:20:29.950
<v Mike Birbiglia>This is the actual report, and it mixes it up so badly that it says P-1, that's me, started to go, but all of a sudden V-1, that's also me, came at a high rate of speed, crashing into him. They're saying I crashed into my own car. I mean I'm pretty self-destructive, but I would never crash into my own car, with my own car. Nor would I understand how to do that.
<v Mike Birbiglia>这是真实的报告，它混淆得太离谱了，上面写着P-1，也就是我，开始行驶，但突然之间V-1，那也是我，以极高的速度冲过来，撞向了他。他们是说我撞了我自己的车。我的意思是，我很善于自我毁灭，但我绝不会用自己的车去撞自己的车。我也根本不明白该怎么做那种事。

00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:57.300
<v Mike Birbiglia>I also love this. The statement of the other driver at the scene of the crime. I was going on Venice. I'm not too sure. I was going away from the beach. I was driving. I don't know what happened. Did I hurt anyone? I don't know where I was going, but I came from home. I had a sip of beer.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我也很喜欢这个。另一位驾驶员在犯罪现场的陈述。我当时在威尼斯大道上行驶。我不太确定。我当时正背离海滩行驶。我在开车。我不知道发生了什么。我伤害到谁了吗？我不知道我要去哪里，但我从家里来。我喝了一口啤酒。

00:20:57.300 --> 00:21:22.640
<v Mike Birbiglia>Which is really everyone's favorite quantity of beer-- is the sip. That's what they serve at the pubs these days-- a pint, a pitcher, a sip, a tablespoon. People are like, aw man, I had a sip of beer. I don't know where I'm driving from, or where I'm driving to.
<v Mike Birbiglia>这真的是每个人最喜欢的啤酒量——一口。这就是现在酒吧里供应的量——一品脱、一大扎、一口、一汤匙。人们就像，噢伙计，我喝了一口啤酒。我不知道我是从哪里开过来的，也不知道我要开到哪里去。

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:46.530
<v Mike Birbiglia>Well, the problem is that even with all that, Officer Timson made one key mistake. He checked the box that said that I was at fault. So I called the police station to get the captain on the phone, and he ducks my calls for days. And I can't get in touch with him, and then finally I get him on the phone. And I'm so relieved. I'm like, I explained that I was wronged, I was nearly killed, and I tell him the whole story.
<v Mike Birbiglia>嗯，问题在于，尽管有这些，蒂姆森警官还是犯了一个关键错误。他勾选了说我有过错的方框。所以我打电话给警察局找队长，他躲了我好几天。我联系不上他，最后终于在电话里找到了他。我感到如释重负。我解释说我受到了冤枉，我差点丢了命，我把整个故事都告诉了他。

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:59.260
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "You made a bad turn, now do the right thing, and pay for the guy's car. I know."
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“你转弯失误了，现在做正确的事，赔那家伙的车吧。我知道。”

00:21:59.260 --> 00:22:15.890
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I explained like this is just an easy thing to fix. Its just a mix up. And he's not hearing any of it. And finally I'm like, please I mean can we just discuss this for a second like just as people, just as like one human being to another?
<v Mike Birbiglia>我解释说这其实很容易解决。这只是个混淆。但他根本听不进去。最后我说，求你了，我的意思是我们可以像普通人一样，像一个人对另一个人那样讨论一下这件事吗？

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:36.300
<v Mike Birbiglia>At this point I'd become Adam Sandler in one of his more sentimental films. I was like can you see how crazy this is? This guy who is drunk crashed into my car, he nearly killed me. I mean inches from where he hit I'd be dead, and you're saying that my parents would have had to pay for his car.
<v Mike Birbiglia>到了这个地步，我简直变成了亚当·桑德勒（Adam Sandler）在某部情感电影里的角色。我说，你能看出这有多疯狂吗？那个醉酒的人撞了我的车，他差点杀了我。我的意思是，如果他撞击的位置离我再近几英寸我就没命了，而你却说我父母必须赔他的车。

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:43.720
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Do the right thing, and pay for the guy's car."
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“做正确的事，赔那家伙的车。”

00:22:43.720 --> 00:23:03.980
<v Mike Birbiglia>And I hang up the phone, and this is when it becomes about principle. This isn't about money. This is about stopping a man who has no regard for people or the law. This is China Town.
<v Mike Birbiglia>我挂断了电话，这时事情就变成了原则问题。这不是钱的问题。这是为了阻止一个不尊重人、不尊重法律的家伙。这就是《唐人街》（China Town）。

00:23:03.980 --> 00:23:34.570
<v Mike Birbiglia>I start printing out Google maps of the scene of the accident and California state driving laws. I pore over the police report, circling inconsistencies, and scrolling notes in the margins. Like, are you kidding me? And this makes no sense and what is this blacked-out part? There was a blacked-out part next to his blood alcohol level. I'm calling lawyers and private investigators. There's only one lawyer who will even consider my case. He's an accident lawyer, and he says, "Did you have any loss of income from the accident?"
<v Mike Birbiglia>我开始打印事故现场的谷歌地图和加州交通法规。我仔细研读警察报告，圈出矛盾之处，在页边空白处写下笔记。就像，你在开玩笑吗？这一切都说不通，还有这个被涂黑的部分是什么？他血液酒精浓度旁边有一个被涂黑的部分。我开始打电话给律师和私人调查员。只有一个律师考虑接我的案子。他是一位事故律师，他说，“事故导致你收入损失了吗？”

00:23:34.570 --> 00:23:36.590
<v Mike Birbiglia>I said, "No."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说，“没有。”

00:23:36.590 --> 00:23:54.140
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he says, "Did you have any loss of income-- from the accident?" And I say, "No, this isn't about money." And it gets very quiet.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他说，“那事故导致你——收入损失了吗？”我说，“没有，这不是钱的问题。”空气变得非常安静。

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:58.150
<v Mike Birbiglia>I said, "I shouldn't have to lie. I'm right."
<v Mike Birbiglia>我说，“我不应该撒谎。我是对的。”

00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:14.090
<v Mike Birbiglia>And he doesn't take the case. And this is when I start going crazy. I start obsessing over the actual driver. I'm like, who is this man who nearly killed me and wants my $12,000? I know his name, it's Jim Bosworth.
<v Mike Birbiglia>他没接这个案子。这时我开始变得疯狂。我开始沉迷于那个驾驶员本身。我想，这个差点杀了我，又想要我12000美元的男人是谁？我知道他的名字，他叫吉姆·博斯沃思（Jim Bosworth）。

00:24:14.090 --> 00:24:47.430
<v Mike Birbiglia>That's not his name, but I do know his name. And I register for an online account at netdetective.com, which is a great site for vigilantes who have $29.95. And so I know where he lives, what he does. My inner monologue becomes like a movie trailer for a revenge thriller like, "Jim Bosworth thought he was going to get away with this, but he didn't count on one thing-- Mike Birbiglia."
