00:37:53.990 --> 00:38:13.690
<v Ira Glass>One of the things that's the strangest thing about returns to the scene of the crime are that so many of them involve somebody returning to someplace where he really doesn't want to go. Someplace that he dislikes, or disagrees with, or wished he'd never been at in the first place. But then the person finds they can't help themselves, he goes back.
<v Ira Glass>关于重返犯罪现场最奇怪的事情之一是，其中很多人都涉及某人回到他根本不想去的地方。他不喜欢、不同意或希望自己从一开始就没去过的地方。但随后那个人发现他无法自拔，他还是回去了。

00:38:13.690 --> 00:38:46.740
<v Ira Glass>Well, Dan Savage has written books about he and his boyfriend deciding to get married and then adopting a little boy, actually not in that order. He made a name for himself writing a funny and deeply informed sex advice column called Savage Love. Please welcome Dan Savage.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，丹·萨维奇写过关于他和男友决定结婚然后收养一个小男孩的书，其实顺序不是那样的。他通过撰写一篇有趣且内涵深刻的性建议专栏“萨维奇之爱”（Savage Love）而声名鹊起。欢迎丹·萨维奇。

00:38:46.740 --> 00:38:53.990
<v Dan Savage>Hello. This is intimidating with that kind of welcome.
<v Dan Savage>你好。这样的欢迎太令人受宠若惊了。

00:38:53.990 --> 00:39:23.950
<v Dan Savage>The crime scene in this story is a Catholic church. In particular, Saint Ignatius on Glenwood Avenue on the North Side of Chicago. And yes, I went to Catholic grade schools. And yes, I was an altar boy. But no, the crime is not what you've already assumed. No Catholic school children were harmed during the production of this story. So you can relax.
<v Dan Savage>这个故事里的犯罪现场是一座天主教堂。具体来说，是芝加哥北区格伦伍德大道上的圣依纳爵教堂（Saint Ignatius）。是的，我上过天主教小学。是的，我是辅祭。但不是，罪行不是你们已经假设的那样。这部故事的制作过程中没有任何天主教学校的学生受到伤害。所以你们可以放心了。

00:39:23.950 --> 00:39:59.100
<v Dan Savage>I'm not a practicing Catholic. I am a lapsed Catholic. An agnostatheist. A sort of agnostic-atheist hybrid. Which means I cross myself on airplanes. I once blew up at a friend who thought it was hilarious to invert one of the crucifixes in my ironic collection of Catholic kitsch. And half the time when I take the Lord's name in vain, like when I mutter Jesus Christ through clenched teeth as my boyfriend passes someone going 90 miles an hour, I am in all honesty seeking the protection of a higher power.
<v Dan Savage>我不是一个虔诚的天主教徒。我是一个“流失”的天主教徒。一个不可知-无神论者。一种不可知论和无神论的混合体。这意味着我在飞机上会画十字。我曾经对一个觉得把我的铁质天主教小摆设收藏中的十字架倒过来很有趣的朋友发火。当我有意或无意提起上帝的名字时，比如当我男友以每小时90英里的速度超车时，我从牙缝里嘟囔着“耶稣基督”，我真心实意地是在寻求更高力量的庇护。

00:39:59.100 --> 00:40:20.890
<v Dan Savage>I go right back to not believing in God once he's safely delivered us back to the right hand lane. Which makes me a hypocrite. And an ingrate. I was raised in a Catholic home. I went to the same Catholic grade school my mother and grandmother did, and I had some of the same nuns they both did as teachers.
<v Dan Savage>一旦他安全地把我们送到正确的车道，我就会立刻回到不相信上帝的状态。这让我成了一个伪君子。一个忘恩负义的人。我是在一个天主教家庭长大的。我上了和我母亲、祖母同一所天主教小学，我还有一些和她们一样的修女老师。

00:40:20.890 --> 00:40:51.830
<v Dan Savage>But by age seven, I was already having trouble reconciling loving father with eternal damnation. But the fatal blow came at age 14, with the realization that I was gay. Something I figured out at roughly the same time I entered an all-male high school for Catholic boys thinking about becoming priests. Which is a bit like realizing you're an alcoholic on your first day working at the Budweiser bottling plant.
<v Dan Savage>但在七岁时，我就已经在调和“仁慈的父亲”与“永恒的诅咒”方面遇到困难了。但致命一击是在14岁时，我意识到自己是同性恋。我意识到这一点的时间大约和我进入一所全是天主教男孩、考虑成为神父的高中是同一时间。这有点像你在百威啤酒灌装厂上班的第一天发现自己是个酒鬼。

00:40:51.830 --> 00:41:29.120
<v Dan Savage>Luckily for my sanity, I didn't think, "Oh my God, I'm going to hell." I thought, that can't be right. What the church-- my church-- says about me. They have to be wrong. Soon I was contemplating the possibility that the church was wrong about other stuff. Maybe lots of other stuff. The odds of virgin births. The virtues of celibacy. The evils of masturbation. And it didn't take long to arrive at the biggest doubt of all-- the existence of God. And that was that.
<v Dan Savage>幸运的是，对于我的理智来说，我没有想，“噢，上帝，我要下地狱了。”我想的是，这肯定不对。我的教会——我的教堂——对我所说的话。他们肯定是错的。不久我就在思考，教会对其他事情也可能是错的。也许是很多其他事情。童贞生子的可能性。独身的美德。自慰的罪恶。而我也没花多久就得出了最大的怀疑——上帝是否存在。就是这样。

00:41:29.120 --> 00:42:24.420
<v Dan Savage>By senior year I'd started going to a public school and stopped going to church, except for the odd family wedding, baptism, or funeral. And they are all odd, aren't they? I go to church about as often as I go to Planned Parenthood for a Pap smear. Then, 12 months ago, my mother died. A virus can lay dormant in your body for so long that it's possible to forget you were ever infected. Then something happens that weakens your immune system and the virus seizes its opportunity. For more than two decades the Catholicism I'd contracted at Saint Ignatius had lain dormant, manifesting itself only on airplanes and in passing lanes. But the immunity I'd long enjoyed was weakened by my mother's death. Because since that sunny, awful day in Tucson last spring, I found myself slipping into Catholic churches.
<v Dan Savage>高年级时我开始去公立学校，不再去教堂，除了偶尔的家庭婚礼、洗礼或葬礼。它们都很奇怪，不是吗？我去教堂的次数和我去计划生育诊所做巴氏涂片检查的次数差不多。然后，12个月前，我母亲去世了。病毒可以在你的体内潜伏很久，以至于你可能忘记自己曾经感染过。然后发生了一些削弱你免疫系统的事情，病毒就趁虚而入。二十多年来，我在圣依纳爵教堂感染的天主教信仰一直处于休眠状态，只在飞机上和超车道上表现出来。但我长期享受的免疫力因母亲的去世而减弱了。因为自从去年春天图森那个阳光明媚、糟糕透顶的日子以来，我发现自己开始溜进天主教堂。

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:53.640
<v Dan Savage>Not for odd weddings or funerals, but on totally random days of non-holy, non-obligation. Tuesday afternoons, Friday mornings. And not just going to church, but going out of my way to go to church. There's a Modernist Catholic chapel close to my office. It won a big design award, but to me it looks like all modern churches do. Like someone stuck a crucifix up on the wall in the rec room at the Brady house.
<v Dan Savage>不是为了奇怪的婚礼或葬礼，而是在完全随机的、非神圣、非义务的日子里。周二下午，周五上午。而且不仅仅是去教堂，而是专门绕道去教堂。我办公室附近有一座现代主义天主教堂。它赢过大设计奖，但在我看来，它看起来像所有的现代教堂一样。就像有人在布雷迪家（Brady house）活动室的墙上贴了一个十字架。

00:42:53.640 --> 00:43:27.600
<v Dan Savage>Saint James Cathedral in downtown Seattle, a longer walk, looks like a Catholic church should. A lot like Saint Ignatius, actually. Stained glass, marble, crowds of plaster saints. Saint James is open for contemplation all day during the week. There are usually one or two volunteers straightening up the hymnals and the offering envelopes in the backs of the pews, and keeping an eye on the homeless people, and me, that have come in to get out of the rain. There's no music, only a few lights are on, and when a priest strolls through he doesn't make eye contact with anyone.
<v Dan Savage>西雅图市中心的圣雅各布大教堂（Saint James Cathedral），路程更远，看起来像一座天主教堂应该有的样子。其实很像圣依纳爵教堂。彩色玻璃、大理石、成群的石膏圣徒。圣雅各布大教堂整周都在开放供沉思。通常有一两个志愿者在整理长椅后面的赞美诗集和捐款信封，并留意着那些进来躲雨的无家可归者，还有我。没有音乐，只有几盏灯亮着，当神父走过时，他不与任何人眼神接触。

00:43:27.600 --> 00:43:59.080
<v Dan Savage>When my mother would call me with bad news-- a relative I hadn't seen in years diagnosed with cancer, a friend of hers with a desperately ill grandchild-- she would always say, "I know you don't pray, Daniel. Keep them in your thoughts." My mom knew that thoughts were the best I could do. And now, at Saint James, I sit in a bank of pews opposite a white marble statue of the Virgin Mother, stare into her face, and keep my mother in my thoughts. Sorry.
<v Dan Savage>每当我母亲带着坏消息打电话给我——一个我多年未见的亲戚被诊断出癌症，或者她朋友的一个孙子病得很重——她总是说，“我知道你不祷告，丹尼尔。把他们放在你的心上。”我妈妈知道“思念”是我能做的最好的事。现在，在圣雅各布大教堂，我坐在长椅上，对面是圣母玛利亚的白色大理石雕像，凝视着她的脸，把母亲放在我的心里。对不起。

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:41.040
<v Dan Savage>By her own estimation my mother was a good Catholic. She believed in Jesus, the Resurrection, the virgin births, both of them. There are two, did you know that? What are the odds? The Trinity, the Sacraments. She believed that sex was sacred, and that people, particularly people with children, should be married to each other. But she didn't believe that being a good Catholic meant blind obedience. So I guess you could say she was a good American Catholic. She believed women should be priests. That priests should be able to marry. And after four pregnancies in four years, she concluded that birth control was not a sin.
<v Dan Savage>按照她自己的说法，我母亲是一个好天主教徒。她相信耶稣，复活，童贞生子，而且是两次。有两个，你知道吗？几率是多少？三位一体，圣礼。她相信性是神圣的，人们，特别是有孩子的人，应该彼此结婚。但她不认为做一个好天主教徒就意味着盲目服从。所以我猜你可以说她是一个典型的美国天主教徒。她相信女性应该成为神父。神父应该可以结婚。在四年内经历了四次怀孕后，她断定节育不是罪。

00:44:41.040 --> 00:45:14.900
<v Dan Savage>She prayed that the leaders of her church would come around during her lifetime. Unfortunately, the church, under the last two popes, went in the opposite direction. And whenever the present pope, Benny, she called him, or the previous pope, JP2, condemned birth control, or insisted that women could not and would not ever be ordained as priests, my mother would sometimes call me and sigh and say, it's like they're trying to make Lutherans of us all.
<v Dan Savage>她祈祷教会领导人在她有生之年能改变主意。不幸的是，在过去两任教皇的领导下，教会走向了相反的方向。每当现任教皇，她称之为“本尼”（Benny），或者前任教皇JP2谴责节育，或者坚持认为女性不能也不会被任命为神父时，我母亲有时会打电话给我，叹气说，就像他们试图把我们都变成路德教徒一样。

00:45:14.900 --> 00:45:45.420
<v Dan Savage>But she refused to leave the church because she believed it was her church, too, just as much as it was Benny's, or JP2's. A little voice inside her head said, that can't be right, they must be wrong. That voice, the same one I heard, somehow left her faith stronger. She took it hard when I came out. Her first impulse was to call a priest. God bless Father Tom, who, when my mother, distraught, told him I'd just come out to her, placed a hand on my mother's knee and came out to her himself.
<v Dan Savage>但她拒绝离开教会，因为她相信这也是她的教堂，和本尼的或JP2的一样多。她脑子里有一个小声音说，那肯定是不对的，他们一定是错的。那个声音，我听到的那个声音，反而使她的信仰更加坚定。我出柜时她很难过。她的第一反应是打电话给神父。上帝保佑汤姆神父，当母亲苦恼地告诉他我刚向她出柜时，他把手放在我母亲的膝盖上，向她坦白他自己也是同性恋。

00:45:45.420 --> 00:45:55.560
<v Dan Savage>[APPLAUSE]
<v Dan Savage>[掌声]

00:45:55.560 --> 00:46:22.480
<v Dan Savage>My mother came around fast. And came out swinging. Rainbow stickers on her car, a PFLAG membership, and an ultimatum delivered to the entire extended family. Anyone who had a problem with me, had a much bigger problem with her.
<v Dan Savage>我母亲很快就想通了。而且开始反击。她的车上贴着彩虹贴纸，加入了PFLAG（同性恋亲友会），并向整个大家庭下了最后通牒。任何对我有意见的人，都会跟她有更大的意见。

00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:47.060
<v Dan Savage>My mother was diagnosed with a degenerative lung condition six years ago. She had some bad years, but she was stable enough last spring to drive down to the Southwest with her husband, my step dad, to visit her sisters. She took a turn in Tucson, where she was hospitalized for a week. But we thought she was going to pull through, as she had on several other occasions. Her doctors in Chicago had just given her two more years.
<v Dan Savage>我母亲六年前被诊断出患有退行性肺部疾病。她经历了几年糟糕的日子，但去年春天病情稳定，足以和她的丈夫、我的继父一起开车去西南部看望她的姐妹们。她在图森病情恶化，在那里住了一周医院。但我们以为她能挺过来，就像她过去几次一样。她在芝加哥的医生刚给了她两年时间。

00:46:47.060 --> 00:47:30.610
<v Dan Savage>But the morning of her seventh day in the ICU, the morning we'd begun to make arrangements to get her back home to her own bed and her own doctors, a moment after my stepfather left her bedside for the first time in a week, a doctor appeared at the door of her room and waved me into the hallway. There'd been developments. Her lungs have been slowly disintegrating for five years, and now they were coming apart. One had a hole in it that was getting bigger, and there was no going home. This was it. The doctor told me he needed a medical directive from my mother, and he needed it now. Can I go get my stepfather? No. Now.
<v Dan Savage>但在ICU的第七天早上，在我们开始安排她回家回到自己的床上看自己的医生时，继父一周来第一次离开床边，一位医生出现在门口，向我招手示意我到走廊去。情况发生了变化。她的肺部五年来一直在缓慢崩解，现在正在分崩离析。其中一个肺有一个洞，而且越来越大，她已经回不去家了。就是现在了。医生告诉我他需要我母亲的医疗指令，而且现在就要。我可以去叫我的继父吗？不。现在。

00:47:30.610 --> 00:48:01.970
<v Dan Savage>So it fell to me to tell my mother that she was going to die, and lay out her limited options. She could have a tube put down her throat and be hooked up to a machine that would breathe for her for a day or two. But she would be in a drug-induced coma, although she would live long enough for her other two sons to come to Tucson, but she wouldn't know that they were there. She could wear an oxygen mask that violently forced air into her lungs and live for six more hours. Or she could take the mask off and go now.
<v Dan Savage>所以告诉母亲她要死了并说明她有限的选择，落在了我头上。她可以插管，连上一台机器呼吸一两天。但她会处于药物诱导的昏迷中，虽然她能活到她的另外两个儿子来到图森，但她不会知道他们在场。她可以戴上强迫空气进入肺部的氧气面罩，再活六个小时。或者她可以摘下面罩，现在就走。

00:48:01.970 --> 00:48:23.060
<v Dan Savage>No mask, she said. No tube. Now. I told the doctor. And then I ran to the ICU's waiting room to get my step dad and my sister and my aunt. She didn't go into the hospital expecting to die, and she was not ready to go.
<v Dan Savage>不戴面罩，她说。不插管。现在。我告诉了医生。然后我跑向ICU的等候室去叫我的继父、姐妹和姨妈。她没想过进医院会死，她也没准备好离开。

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:34.610
<v Dan Savage>When we were all at her bedside, she arched one eyebrow, shook her head and said, "Shit!"
<v Dan Savage>当我们都站在床边时，她挑起一根眉毛，摇了摇头说，“Shit（该死）！”

00:48:34.610 --> 00:49:12.520
<v Dan Savage>My mother used profanity sparingly, and only in quotation marks. When she said "shit," what she meant was the kind of person who casually uses profanity might be inclined to say "shit" at a moment like this, but I am not the kind of person who uses profanity. I am a Catholic grandmother. And I certainly wouldn't use it at a moment like this, but if I were the kind of person who used profanity, "shit" might be the word I would use right now. But I'm not that kind of person. She swore on her death bed as a joke, because she wanted to make us feel better.
<v Dan Savage>我母亲很少使用亵渎语言，而且只在引用时使用。当她说“shit”时，她的意思是，那种随便使用亵渎语言的人可能会在这个时候说“shit”，但我不是那种使用亵渎语言的人。我是一个天主教祖母。我绝对不会在这个时候使用它，但如果我是那种使用亵渎语言的人，“shit”可能就是我现在会用的词。但我不是那种人。她在临终前开玩笑地咒骂，因为她想让我们感觉好受点。

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:51.100
<v Dan Savage>A priest arrived to perform last rites. And it helped her brace for what she knew was coming for an extremely painful final few moments. I could see it help. I could see the comfort the sacrament gave her. It gave me comfort. The priest led us in prayer. Prayers my mother and I both learned at Saint Ignatius. Prayers that filled a terrible silence, and solemnized an awful moment. And then my mother told us she would be with us always, looking down on us, and that we would see her again. She didn't say in heaven, but that is what she meant.
<v Dan Savage>一位神父赶来做临终圣事。这帮助她坚强起来，迎接她知道即将来临的极其痛苦的最后几分钟。我能看到这很有帮助。我能看到圣礼给她带来的安慰。它也给了我安慰。神父带领我们祈祷。我和母亲在圣依纳爵教堂学会的祈祷。这些祈祷填补了可怕的沉默，并使这个可怕的时刻变得庄重。然后母亲告诉我们她会永远和我们在一起，注视着我们，我们还会再见到她。她没说在天堂，但那是她的意思。

00:49:51.100 --> 00:50:01.240
<v Dan Savage>Then she made her last request. "Remember me. Keep me in your thoughts, Daniel."
<v Dan Savage>然后她提出了最后一个要求。“记住我。把我放在心里，丹尼尔。”

00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:26.100
<v Dan Savage>One of the cards in the back of the pews at Saint James is addressed to non-practicing Catholics. "WELCOME BACK" is printed in large letters across the top. Are you a Catholic who's been away from the church? Welcome back classes are designed to help you return to the sacraments and regular church attendance. A return to the sacraments.
<v Dan Savage>圣雅各布大教堂长椅后的卡片之一是写给“非虔诚天主教徒”的。“欢迎回来”几个大字印在顶部。你是离开教会的天主教徒吗？“欢迎回来”课程旨在帮助你重返圣礼和常规礼拜。重返圣礼。

00:50:26.100 --> 00:50:49.280
<v Dan Savage>I fantasized about returning to the sacrament of confession. Forgive me father, for I have sinned. It's been 29 years since my last confession. Hope you packed a lunch.
<v Dan Savage>我幻想过重返忏悔圣礼。宽恕我，神父，因为我犯了罪。距离我上次忏悔已经29年了。希望你准备好午餐了。

00:50:49.280 --> 00:51:18.810
<v Dan Savage>I want for there to be heaven. And I want her to be looking down on me. Though, and I say this is a professional sex advice columnist, not all the time. There are things a mother has a right not to know, she used to say. But Catholicism now tempts me. I wouldn't be wasting so much time in Saint James if it didn't. My boyfriend would not have found a "WELCOME BACK" card in the back pocket of my jeans if it didn't.
<v Dan Savage>我希望有天堂。我希望她在注视着我。虽然，我是以一名专业性建议专栏作家的身份这么说，而不是一直看着。她过去常说，有些事情母亲有权不知道。但天主教现在诱惑着我。如果不是这样，我就不会在圣雅各布大教堂浪费这么多时间了。如果不是这样，我的男友就不会在我牛仔裤的后口袋里发现一张“欢迎回来”的卡片了。

00:51:18.810 --> 00:51:45.760
<v Dan Savage>But when I am tempted, when I feel like, maybe, I could go through the motions, Return to the sacraments, take what comfort I can, the Pope goes to Africa and says that condoms spread AIDS, or an archbishop in Brazil excommunicates a Catholic woman for getting her nine year old daughter an abortion, but not the Catholic man that raped the nine year old girl.
<v Dan Savage>但当我受到诱惑时，当我感觉，也许，我可以走走过场，重返圣礼，获得我可以获得的安慰时，教皇去了非洲说避孕套传播艾滋病，或者巴西的一位大主教因一名天主教女性为她9岁的女儿堕胎而将她逐出教会，却并没有那样对待强奸那9岁女孩的天主教男子。

00:51:45.760 --> 00:52:01.770
<v Dan Savage>Or I contemplate how the church views me and the two people I love most in the world, my boyfriend of 14 years and our 11-year-old son, and I think, I can't even fake this. But there I sit at Saint James two or three times a week.
<v Dan Savage>或者我想到教会是如何看待我和我生命中最爱的两个人——我相处14年的男友和我们11岁的儿子——我就想，我甚至连假装都做不到。但我还是每周两三次坐在圣雅各布大教堂。

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:35.350
<v Dan Savage>Being brought up in a faith built around a guy jumping out of his tomb? That makes it difficult to reconcile oneself to the permanence of death. Who knew? The afterlife. It's cruel really, when you think about it. Criminal. Telling children that the people they love don't die. That there's some other life, some better place, a place without pulmonary fibrosis, or Tucson, Arizona.
<v Dan Savage>在这样一个围绕着一个跳出坟墓的家伙的信仰中长大？这使得人们很难接受死亡的永久性。谁知道呢？来世。当你想到它时，这真的很残酷。犯罪。告诉孩子们他们爱的人不会死。告诉他们还有另一种生活，一个更好的地方，一个没有肺纤维化、没有图森、亚利桑那州的地方。

00:52:35.350 --> 00:53:02.480
<v Dan Savage>And maybe that lie is a comfort for some, but it's made death more painful for me, not less. Which is the opposite of religion's intended effect, is it not? The voice of unreason in my head, the voice of nuns back at Saint Ignatius says, "She lives. She is in heaven." And the voice of reason, which sounds a lot like Christopher Hitchens, barks back, "No, she doesn't. She's dead. Get over it already."
<v Dan Savage>也许那个谎言对某些人来说是一种安慰，但它让我对死亡感到更加痛苦，而不是更少。这难道不是宗教预期效果的反面吗？我脑子里的非理性声音，圣依纳爵教堂修女的声音说，“她活着。她在天堂。”而理性声音，听起来很像克里斯托弗·希钦斯（Christopher Hitchens），反驳道，“不，她没有。她死了。快点走出来吧。”

00:53:02.480 --> 00:53:27.170
<v Dan Savage>This inability to reconcile myself to death has not been good for me. I visit Saint James like an addict drops by a crack house. For a fix. To deaden the pain, by losing myself momentarily in the fantasy that she lives, and that we will be together again. There's an inscription on the ceiling of Saint James, "I am in your midst."
<v Dan Savage>这种无法调和死亡的心态对我来说并不好。我访问圣雅各布大教堂就像吸毒者访问毒窟一样。为了寻找慰藉。通过迷失在“她活着，我们会再次在一起”的幻想中，来减轻痛苦。圣雅各布大教堂的天花板上有一段铭文，“我在你们中间。”

00:53:27.170 --> 00:53:39.880
<v Dan Savage>If I were the kind of person who could believe, I would believe. But I'm not that kind of person. Shit. Thank you.
<v Dan Savage>如果我是那种能相信的人，我就会相信。但我不是那种人。Shit。谢谢。

00:53:39.880 --> 00:53:45.810
<v Dan Savage>[APPLAUSE]
<v Dan Savage>[掌声]

00:53:45.810 --> 00:53:58.130
<v Ira Glass>Dan Savage. Sex advice columnist. Editor of The Stranger. And the host of one of the most entertaining podcasts in the country, The Savage Love podcast.
<v Ira Glass>丹·萨维奇。性建议专栏作家。《The Stranger》编辑。以及全国最有趣的播客之一，“萨维奇之爱”播客的主持人。

00:53:58.130 --> 00:54:16.660
<v Ira Glass>A couple weeks ago as we were preparing for today's broadcast, I was in Baltimore visiting my dad, and I'd been going over drafts of the stories for today's show including Dan's, and I was visiting my dad in Baltimore. And my dad showed me how he had digitized all of our old family videos, which apparently is a really good project if you're retired.
<v Ira Glass>几周前当我们准备今天的广播时，我在巴尔的摩看望我爸爸，我一直在审阅今天节目故事的草稿，包括丹的故事，我当时在巴尔的摩看望我父亲。我父亲向我展示了他如何将我们所有的老家庭录像数字化，这显然是一个退休后的好项目。

00:54:16.660 --> 00:54:52.480
<v Ira Glass>And among the videos was footage from one of our very first live shows. We'd just barely been on the air at all, and it was Valentine's Day in 1998. It was a slightly smaller audience than we have today. It was at a bar. The Empty Bottle on Western in Chicago. The Empty Bottle's reach is that big? Anyway so he had footage. He had filmed the thing, and there was this footage that I didn't even know existed, and had never seen.
<v Ira Glass>录像中包括我们最早的现场表演之一的片段。我们几乎刚开播，那是1998年的情人节。观众比今天少一点。那是在一个酒吧。芝加哥西部大道上的Empty Bottle。“Empty Bottle”的影响力有那么大吗？总之他有录像。他拍了这个东西，有这个片段，我甚至不知道它存在，也从未见过。

00:54:52.480 --> 00:55:12.640
<v Ira Glass>And onstage was me and my mom. My mom died five years ago. She was a marriage therapist and she was giving Valentine's Day relationship advice. And on stage with her was Dan Savage who was giving relationship and sex advice. And then at some point, Dan introduces a guest of his own. Let's roll that.
<v Ira Glass>台上是我和我妈。我妈五年前去世了。她是一位婚姻治疗师，她在给情人节关系建议。和她一起在台上的是丹·萨维奇，他正在提供关系和性建议。然后在某个时候，丹介绍了他的嘉宾。让我们播放那一段。

00:55:12.640 --> 00:55:13.040
